cartridge "SHIFTER/Kevin Mykytyn and Mark Tuttle/1986"

' SHIFTER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Shifter" by Kevin Mykytyn and Mark Tuttle,
' COMPUTE!'s Gazette, March 1986, page 40 (Commodore 64 / Plus/4 BASIC
' with a 854-byte machine-language core at $3C00, disk 1986-03). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from the
' BASIC listing and the disassembly, orig/ml.dis, and watched in jsc64),
' rebuilt for the ColecoVision: a smooth ball sprite, highlight tiles,
' cursor arrows on the frame, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' From the original (orig/shifter.bas, orig/ml.dis):
' - two players share a square board of mirror lines (each cell: one of
'   - | \ / with 1 chance in 28 each, else empty) designed at random for
'   each new game and kept from round to round;
' - ROWS (yellow; C64 joystick port 2) moves a row cursor up and down and
'   slides that whole row left or right, wrapping around; COLUMNS (red;
'   port 1) moves a column cursor left and right and slides that column
'   up or down; nobody can slide the row (ROWS) or the column (COLUMNS)
'   the ball is in;
' - each round a ball starts in the middle in a random direction and
'   runs one cell at a time: - turns back a ball going up or down, | one
'   going left or right, \ and / turn it a quarter, as mirrors;
' - a ball leaving by a side gives ROWS a point, by the top or bottom
'   COLUMNS a point; the scorer's frame edges flash 8 times;
' - first to 5 points wins; the board stays between rounds;
' - level 1 FAST, 2, 3 SLOW: the ball moves every 3/4/5 passes of the
'   main loop (measured in jsc64: about 9 and 18 jiffies a cell for
'   levels 1 and 3).
'
' Liberties: the board is 19 x 19 (the C64's 23 x 23 does not fit the
' TV-safe 22 rows); speeds 10/14/18 frames a cell (FAST/MEDIUM/SLOW); the
' ball glides between cells; a held stick repeats after 12 frames every 5
' (the C64 repeated every pass, about 4 frames); one player against a
' computer COLUMNS that looks ahead along the ball's path, one column
' slide at a time, about 40 frames late, moving its cursor every 6
' frames; a ball still on the board after 200 cells ends the round with no
' point (DRAW) so a ball trapped between mirrors cannot stall the game;
' the end names the winner (the C64 said GAME OVER); the middle cell is
' always empty (on the C64 the ball's first move erased it).
'
' Controls: controller 1 is ROWS: up/down move the row cursor, left/right
' slide the row. Controller 2 is COLUMNS: left/right move the column
' cursor, up/down slide the column. Title: up/down choose a line,
' left/right change it, a button picks (START, HOW TO PLAY, CREDITS).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

